Nice dataset!  I've drawn some plots of it:

  http://vyznev.net/misc/deathbyrolelevelnorm.png
  http://vyznev.net/misc/deathbyrolelevelnorm.svg

The most obvious feature in the plot is the peak at dlvl 1 and the
corresponding trough at dlvl 2, followed by a second peak at dlvls
3-5.  Clearly, if you've survived the first level, you can expect to
survive the second one too, but then the game starts getting harder
again.  If it weren't for that first-level peak, one could almost
pretend that the depth of death is approximately Poisson-distributed.

Oddly enough, Rangers are the only class with less deaths on dlvl 1
than on dlvl 2, even though their death rates otherwise seem to be
close to the median.  I guess the starting arrows really do help.
Also, Monks are clear outliers, with quite a few first-level deaths
but fewer than any other class on dlvls 2-5.

Another puzzling feature is that some classes, especially Monk and
Valk, have a noticeable bump in the death rate from dlvl 10 to around
dlvl 13.  I first thought that might be the Quest, but that only comes
later; Mines' End is in that range, as is the Big Room, but neither
_seems_ likely to cause such a class-specific bump.

I'd like to do a cumulative plot too, but for that I'd need the number
of ascensions for each class from the same sample too -- otherwise the
plot would make it seems like nobody survives the Sanctum.  Any chance
of getting those?

I also did some logarithmic plots to show the latter part of the game
better:

  http://vyznev.net/misc/deathbyrolelevelnormlog.png
  http://vyznev.net/misc/deathbyrolelevelnormlog.svg

The breaks in the lines between dlvls 30 to 45 reflect the fact that
many classes had zero deaths on many of those levels.

